2109375, -0.0235748291015625, -0.00803375244140625, -0.02392578125, -0.0260772705078125, -0.0248260498046875, -0.0304107666015625, 0.045135498046875, 0.0220184326171875, 0.016998291015625, 0.0142822265625, 0.0163421630859375, 0.00615692138671875, 0.0020847320556640625, -0.05255126953125, 0.00742340087890625, -0.0010747909545898438, 0.0057830810546875, 0.039398193359375, -0.022705078125, -0.0007424354553222656, -0.0187225341796875, -0.0094757080078125, -0.005218505859375, -0.003627777099609375, 0.00940704345703125, 0.0157623291015625, 0.0017900466918945312, 0.0172119140625, -0.0161895751953125, 0.0235137939453125, -0.006633758544921875, 0.0208892822265625, -0.0084381103515625, -0.0001016855239868164, 0.0056915283203125, -0.021484375, -0.004062652587890625, -0.0113372802734375, -0.01397705078125, -0.021331787109375, -0.0151824951171875, 0.0160980224609375, -0.03839111328125, 0.01175689697265625, 0.005962371826171875, 0.01153564453125, -0.010772705078125, -0.005859375, -0.0162506103515625, 0.0057220458984375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "How level-0 players behave and how they are perceived by higher-level players are central questions in the literature on level-k models of boundedly rational strategic reasoning. This study addresses two main research questions: (1) How are level-0 actions and beliefs formed, and do they align within individuals? (2) How do these patterns differ across games, and what are the implications for the assumptions underlying standard level-k models? We hypothesize that the alignment between level-0 actions and beliefs is context-dependent and that standard level-k assumptions may not hold uniformly across different strategic settings.\n\nTo investigate these questions, we employ the intra-team communication method developed by Burchardi and Penczynski (2014). In this approach, participants are grouped into teams and required to reach a consensus on their choices through open discussion. These discussions are recorded and analyzed to elicit the reasoning processes underlying decisions, allowing us to identify which actions and beliefs correspond to level-0 reasoning. This method is particularly suitable for distinguishing level-0 behavior because it makes explicit the thought processes that would otherwise remain unobserved, enabling a more accurate classification of non-strategic choices and beliefs.\n\nOur study involves two experimental games, each conducted with independent samples of undergraduate students recruited from a university subject pool. In total, 120 participants (60 per game) took part in the experiments. Participants were randomly assigned to teams of three, and each team played the assigned game after receiving standardized instructions. Demographic information was collected, with the majority of participants aged 18\u201325 and balanced in gender representation.\n\nFirst, in the canonical beauty contest game, players are asked to choose a number between 0 and 100, aiming to guess two-thirds of the average number chosen by all participants. Burchardi and Penczynski (2014) previously found that elicited level-0 beliefs closely match observed level-0 actions, supporting the standard level-k assumption that level-0 players act non-strategically and are perceived as such by others. In our replication, 18% of teams (11 out of 60 participants) exhibited level-0 reasoning, with a mean chosen number of 50 (SD = 2.1), and their stated beliefs about others\u2019 choices also centered around 50. This quantitative alignment confirms the correspondence between beliefs and actions for level-0 players in this context.\n\nSecond, we study a variant of the 11-20 game, originally introduced by Goeree et al. (2018). In the standard version, each player selects an integer between 11 and 20, and a player receives a payoff only if their chosen number is exactly one less than another player's choice. In our variant, we modified the payoff structure so that the reward for choosing a number exactly one less than another\u2019s choice was doubled, and a small bonus was added for choosing the lowest unique number, thereby increasing the incentive for strategic reasoning while retaining the core mechanics. In this game, 15% of teams (9 out of 60 participants) were classified as exhibiting level-0 reasoning based on their discussions. However, their observed actions (mean = 15.2, SD = 2.8) diverged from their stated beliefs about others\u2019 choices (mean = 17.6, SD = 1.9), and both differed from the standard level-0 assumption of choosing the lowest available number (11). This quantitative divergence suggests that the standard level-0 specification may not fully capture the reasoning processes in this setting and points to the need for alternative or additional explanations beyond those previously proposed in the literature.\n\nThe observed divergence between beliefs and actions in the 11-20 game variant is significant for level-k modeling because it challenges the assumption that level-0 behavior is both non-strategic and commonly understood by higher-level players. Our findings suggest that level-0 reasoning may be influenced by contextual cues and payoff structures, and that higher-level players may misperceive or mispredict level-0 behavior in certain games. To refine level-k models, researchers might consider incorporating context-dependent definitions of level-0 behavior or allowing for heterogeneity in non-strategic reasoning across games. Alternative explanations, such as the influence of focal points or heuristic-based reasoning, could also be explored to account for observed discrepancies.\n\nIn summary, our study demonstrates that the alignment between level-0 beliefs and actions is context-dependent, supporting standard assumptions in some games but not others, and underscores the need for more nuanced modeling of boundedly rational behavior in strategic settings. Limitations of our study include the use of a student sample and a limited set of games, which may affect generalizability. Future research could examine a broader range of games, participant populations, and alternative elicitation methods to further clarify the formation and perception of level-0 reasoning."}}